# D12. HashMap and HashSet iterate in the JDK's order.
# Provenance. Written for the clean room (D14) by a fresh subagent (Claude Opus 5.5) that opened no OpenJDK
# source. Its inputs were programs run on the pinned JDK (every rule below was derived from them, and
# 7c90c06, the commit that adds this patch, names the experiment behind each one), TeaVM's own THashMap,
# THashSet and TLinkedHashMap, and the textbook (CLRS) red-black algorithms; fix round 1 (081ed1e, a second
# clean-room subagent, Opus 5.5) had the same inputs plus the review finding it answers. 7c90c06 also lists
# the public Javadoc, but no Javadoc page was opened, so it was not an input. The first version's internal
# names (balanceInsertion, balanceDeletion, moveRootToFront, treeify, untreeify) matched names in OpenJDK's
# HashMap, as a reviewer recognized them from memory without opening that source; 081ed1e renamed them. A
# later review noted that two names still here match OpenJDK's as well: unlinkEntry's parameter movable
# and the tree entry's field red. As 7c90c06 says, the implementer is a language model and may have met
# descriptions of OpenJDK's HashMap in training, so the shape of some hypotheses may come from there; no
# rule was taken on that basis, and each was kept only after an experiment on the JDK picked it over its
# rivals. An executable model of these rules agreed with java.util.HashMap on 20,000 random operation
# sequences, and the patched fork with the JDK on 100 random programs (700 scenarios, the order printed
# after every operation).
#   - Bucket: (h ^ (h >>> 16)) & (capacity - 1), h the key's hashCode (0 for null).
#   - put and putIfAbsent add a new key at the end of its bucket and double the table after adding it once
#     size > loadFactor * capacity. merge, compute and computeIfAbsent add it at the front and double before
#     they look whenever size > threshold (even for a present key or a null result), never after; a function
#     that adds or removes keys throws ConcurrentModificationException. Doubling keeps each bucket's order.
#   - Capacity: HashMap() 16, HashMap(n) the next power of two >= n (1 for 0), settled by the first insertion.
#     putAll into an unsettled map asks for the next power of two >= ceil(size / loadFactor) (a larger request
#     stands); into a settled one it doubles while its size alone passes the threshold. new HashMap<>(m) and
#     clone() are HashMap() then putAll. new HashSet<>(c) starts at max(ceil(size / 0.75), 16). The
#     clone() rule is untested: HashMap.clone() traps on the fork, so no gate has run it.
#   - A new key that makes a bucket hold 9 keys (8 for the compute family) doubles a table of fewer than 64
#     buckets; otherwise the bucket becomes a red-black tree ordered by hash (signed), then compareTo for keys
#     of one Comparable class. The bucket's list stays the iteration order: the tree is built in list order
#     and its root moves to the front; a new key goes right after its parent and the root moves to the front
#     again. map.remove turns a small tree (its root has no left child, or that child has none) back into a
#     list; otherwise it deletes by the successor and moves the root to the front. Removal through an
#     iterator does neither. On doubling, a tree part of 6 keys or fewer becomes a list, a part that took all
#     of the keys stays as it was, and any other part is built again.
#   - Known limit, as far as it was observed: for colliding keys of one class that does not implement
#     Comparable, the JDK's order moved with unrelated allocations (seen on the JDK itself), so no rule
#     can match it; here such a tie goes right. Other mixes of key classes were not tried.
#   - LinkedHashMap keeps its own order: it uses the new bucket function and keeps doing merge, compute and
#     computeIfAbsent through get, put and remove.
#
# Applies to: github.com/konsoletyper/teavm tag 0.13.1 (b3a245b7), after teavm-0001 to teavm-0010; it
# touches no line an earlier patch added (no earlier patch touches THashMap, THashSet or TLinkedHashMap), so
# the patch applies in every leave-one-out build, which leaves out one patch and the patches that declare
# they build on it.

